     "body": "## Problem\n\nSettings → Privacy → **Find with a local model** always runs the bundled `pf-serve` on the machine magpie runs on, with the device magpie picks. There is no way to choose where or how the model runs.\n\nOn an Intel Mac (Xeon E5-2698B v3, AMD Radeon RX 5600 XT) this breaks the whole gateway:\n\n- `pf-serve-darwin-amd64` is built CPU-only (no Metal backend; the hello line says `\"gpuError\":\"no usable 'gpu' device (built with PF_CUDA/PF_VULKAN? driver present?)\"`), even though the GPU supports Metal 3.\n- With several coding agents running, every request's full context goes through the model on the CPU. `pf-serve` sat at 1300–2200% CPU, load average went past 60, and **every** route (claude/, codex/, antigravity/, zcode/, workbuddy/) timed out at 45–60s. Turning the model off fixed it immediately.\n\nBenchmark with the same 176 KB text through the pf-serve stdin protocol (same `privacy-filter-q8.gguf`, same 329 spans found):\n\n| where | device | time |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intel Mac, `pf-serve` (darwin-amd64) | CPU | 238.7 s |\n| Windows PC on the LAN, `pf-serve-vulkan.exe` | Vulkan, RX 6750 XT | 3.3 s |\n\n## Workaround we're running\n\nBecause `pf-serve` is a plain stdin/stdout process, we replaced the Mac binary in `~/.cache/magpie/privacy-filter/pf-v1-darwin-amd64/pf-serve` with a shell script that `exec`s `ssh <windows-host> \"pf-serve-vulkan.exe privacy-filter-q8.gguf\"` (falling back to the original binary when the host is unreachable). It works end to end: masking records show `model`-found PERSON/ADDRESS again, requests are back to normal latency, and the Mac's load dropped from ~58 to ~13.\n\nBut it is fragile: any re-download/update of pf-serve silently puts the CPU binary back, and nothing in Settings shows the model is remote.\n\n## Request: let users configure the masking model\n\nAny of these would solve it (roughly in order of how general they are):\n\n1. **Custom pf-serve command** — a setting (e.g. `privacyModel.command`) whose argv magpie spawns instead of the bundled binary, speaking the same stdin/stdout protocol. This alone allows SSH to a GPU box, a self-built Metal binary, a container, etc.\n2. **Remote endpoint** — point magpie at a `pf-serve` reachable over TCP/HTTP (one GPU host shared by several machines running magpie), with a timeout and a choice of what happens when it doesn't answer (skip the model / fall back to local / hold the request).\n3. **Device and model choice** — expose `PF_DEVICE` / thread count in Settings, allow a custom GGUF path (e.g. another quantization of the same model), and ship a Metal build for darwin-amd64 so Intel Macs with AMD GPUs can use them.\n4. **Pluggable detector (longer term)** — a documented interface (the current span format: start/end byte offsets, score, label) so a different PII/NER model can be plugged in, related to the \"optional local-model detection\" part of #1977.\n\nAlso worth considering independently: when the model runs on the CPU, a concurrency limit / per-request timeout so a slow model degrades masking instead of stalling every route.\n\nEnvironment: magpie 0.1.1486, macOS 26 (Darwin 25.6.0, x86_64), pf-v1 release assets.\n",
